course: language
lesson: 28
title: Puns and jokes that need a pun
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A pun is a joke that uses a word or sound with two meanings at once, so your brain flips between them and the flip is what makes you laugh (or groan).
explain: Grandad strokes his beard: "I used to hate facial hair, but then it grew on me"; "grew on me" means "I started to like it", and a beard also really grows on you.
explain: To get a pun, hunt for the word with two meanings or two words that sound alike: "seven eight nine" sounds like "seven ate nine".
explain: Puns hardly ever survive translation, because the two meanings live in one language's words; a translator has to invent a new pun instead.
explain: A pun that makes everyone groan has still worked; the groan is part of the fun, which is the whole secret of dad jokes.
fact: A pun is a joke using a word or phrase with two meanings, or two words that sound the same; a bad pun that makes people groan is called a groaner.
fact: "It grew on me" means "I started to like it", and in the beard joke it also means the hair literally grew.
fact: "Two tired" in the bicycle joke sounds like "too tired", and a bike has two tyres (spelled "tires" in America).
fact: "A fish with no eyes is a fsh": take away the i, which sounds like "eye", and "fish" becomes "fsh".
fact: In "Time flies like an arrow", "flies" is what time does and "like" means "in the same way as"; in "Fruit flies like a banana", fruit flies are small insects and "like" means "enjoy".
fact: In the knock-knock joke, "Lettuce in" sounds like "let us in".
fact: Puns usually depend on sounds or double meanings in one language, so when translated the double meaning disappears.
fact: "Impossible to put down" usually means very exciting to read; in the anti-gravity joke it also means the book floats away.
fact: "Why was 6 afraid of 7? Because seven eight nine" works because "eight" and "ate" are homophones.
fact: "De-brie" sounds like "debris", the broken bits left after something breaks; brie is a soft French cheese.
fact: A dad joke is a simple, corny pun, like "I'm afraid for the calendar, its days are numbered"; part of the fun is that it's so bad.
exercise: Grandad says "I used to hate facial hair, but then it grew on me." What are the two meanings? | He came to like it, and beards grow on you | He hated it, and then he shaved the whole thing off for good | He grew a beard and then hated it | He likes gardening, and grows plants on his windowsill
exercise: What is a pun? | A joke using a word with two meanings or a sound-alike word | Any joke about animals that talk, like a parrot or a fish | A very long story | A joke that must be completely true, or else it is a lie
exercise: Why couldn't the bicycle stand up by itself? | It was "two tired": two tyres, too tired | It had no pedals, so it couldn't balance at all | It was too heavy | It had a flat seat
exercise: What do you call a fish with no eyes? | A fsh | A blind fish | A fishy | A shellfish
exercise: In "Fruit flies like a banana", what are "fruit flies"? | Small insects | Fruit that flies through the air | Bananas with wings | Pilots who eat fruit
exercise: In "Time flies like an arrow", what does "like" mean? | In the same way as | Enjoy | Love | Want
exercise: "Lettuce in, it's cold out here!" What does "Lettuce in" sound like? | Let us in | Let's eat in | Let it in | Lots of salad
exercise: Liam's Ukrainian friend asks why his English pun doesn't work in Ukrainian. What's the reason? | The double meaning is in the English words and gets lost | Ukrainian has no jokes at all, so there is nothing to translate it into | She doesn't like jokes | Puns only work when shouted
exercise: Grace's anti-gravity book is "impossible to put down". What are the two meanings? | Very exciting to read, and it floats away | Very heavy to lift, and very boring to read | Too big for the shelf, and too small | Badly written, and torn
exercise: Why was 6 afraid of 7? | Because "seven eight nine" sounds like "seven ate nine" | Because 7 is bigger than 6, and big numbers always scare small ones | Because 7 is an odd number | Because 6 can't count
exercise: The Belfast boy says only "de-brie" was left after the cheese factory exploded. What word is hidden? | Debris | Debit | Debate | Breeze
exercise: What is brie? | A soft French cheese | A sea breeze | A kind of bread | A Belfast street
exercise: What is a dad joke? | A simple, corny pun | A joke only dads can understand | A joke about dads | A rude joke
exercise: Why does the calendar's "days are numbered" joke work? | "Days are numbered" means it won't last long, and a calendar's days really have numbers | Calendars are scary | It's about a broken calendar | Numbers are funny by themselves
exercise: When people groan at a pun, what does it usually mean? | The pun worked | The pun failed completely | Nobody understood it | The joke was rude
test: Your mate goes "puns are only for brainy kids, I don't get 'em, bruv." What's the best tip? | Look for the word with two meanings or a sound-alike word | Only read jokes in dictionaries, because that's where puns are explained | Puns can't be learned | Laugh even if you don't get it
test: Your cousin translates "Lettuce in, it's cold" into Ukrainian word for word. What will happen? | The pun disappears, because "lettuce" and "let us" only sound alike in English | It will be just as funny, because a good joke works the same in every language | It will be funnier | It will turn into a different joke by itself
test: What would happen to the "seven eight nine" joke if "ate" and "eight" sounded different? | The joke would stop working | It would be twice as funny | It would become a maths question | Nothing would change
test: A Gen Z teen says "dad jokes are mid, they're not real jokes." ("Mid" means average, not great.) What's fairer? | They are real jokes; the groan is part of the fun | She's right, they can't be jokes if nobody laughs | Only mums can tell them | They're jokes only in Ireland
test: Sam reads "Fruit flies like a banana" and thinks fruit can fly. What's the trick? | "Fruit flies" are insects, and "like" means enjoy | Bananas really can fly when you throw them hard | It's a spelling mistake | "Like" means "as fast as"
test: Your nan from Cornwall says "proper job, that bike joke, my lover." ("Proper job" means well done, "my lover" is a friendly greeting.) Which word pair makes the bike joke work? | Two and too | Bike and bake | Stand and sand | Tired and tied
test: A cartoon fish with no eyes says "I'm a fish!" What's the punchline it missed? | Without the i (eye), it's a "fsh" | It's a "shell" | It's a "blind fish" | It's a "fishy"
test: Your friend says "a joke has to be true, otherwise it's a lie." What's true? | Puns are fun because both meanings work at once | He's right, all jokes must be true or they're just lies | Jokes are lies and should be banned | Only true jokes make people laugh
test: Make your own pun: which word has two meanings you could use for a joke about a tree? | Bark | Leaf green | Oak tall | Root deep
test: Billy's mate in Liverpool says "that six and seven joke's boss, la." ("Boss" means great, "la" means mate.) Which two words sound the same in it? | Eight and ate | Six and seven | Afraid and fried | Nine and fine
test: Which sentence hides a pun? | The calendar's days are numbered | The calendar is on the wall | The calendar has twelve months | The calendar was a present
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a pun? | A humorous use of a word bearing two meanings, or of words identical in sound | A narrative of considerable length concerning animals endowed with the power of speech | A statement that must be literally true | A joke composed exclusively of numerals
exam: Identify the principal reason for which puns are seldom preserved in translation. | The double meaning depends upon the words of a particular language | Translators are forbidden by professional regulation to render any form of humour | Puns exist in only one language worldwide | Translation removes all words of one syllable
exam: In respect of the jest "Why was six afraid of seven? Because seven eight nine", select the response consistent with the established facts. | The humour arises from the homophones "eight" and "ate" | The humour arises from an error in counting | The humour arises from seven being an odd number | The jest contains no wordplay whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the two senses operative in the statement that a book on anti-gravity is "impossible to put down". | That it is engrossing to read and that it would literally float | That it is heavy to lift and that it is excessively expensive to purchase | That it is lost and that it is stolen | That it is boring and that it is short
